from ./Escapes import Escapes
from ./Layout import Layout

export anchor Values:
    description:
        Values are what follow the colon, both in a component's properties and
        in an instance's attributes. This section sets out how a value is
        split into parts, and which units and colour formats Emul accepts.

    propertyValues:
        A property's value is everything after the colon to the end of the
        line, with the spaces around it trimmed. A value can't continue onto
        the next line.

    splitting:
        Within a value, parts are separated as follows:

        - spaces
            Separate the parts of a single value, such as the four sides in
            `padding: 8px 12px 8px 12px`. Any run of spaces counts as one.
        - commas
            Separate whole items in properties that take a list, such as
            several shadows in `boxShadow`, or several typefaces in
            `fontFamily`.
        - slashes
            Separate the halves of properties whose entry says so, such as
            `aspectRatio: 16 / 9` or `gridColumn: 1 / 3`. Spaces around the
            slash are optional.
        - parentheses
            Group the arguments of a function, such as `rgba()` or
            `repeat()`. Commas and slashes inside parentheses belong to the
            function and do not split the value.
        - quotes
            Group text that contains spaces, such as a typeface name like
            `"Helvetica Neue"`, or the row strings of `gridTemplateAreas`.
            Commas, slashes and parentheses inside quotes are just text.

    bodyEntries:
        In a composition, a body's entries are separated by commas or line
        breaks. Only a comma at the top level of the body ends an entry. A
        comma inside quotes or parentheses does not, so quoted text and
        quoted attribute values can contain commas freely. A comma in an
        unquoted attribute value must be escaped, as described in
        @{Escapes}.

    units:
        Sizes are given in exactly two units:

        - px
            Pixels, written as a number followed by `px`, such as `12px` or
            `0.5px`.
        - percent
            A percentage, written as a number followed by `%`, such as `50%`.
            What it is a percentage of is set by each property's entry.

        Wherever a property's entry lists `<length>`, it means a value in
        `px`. A percentage is only accepted where the entry also lists
        `<percentage>`. Other CSS units, such as `em`, `rem`, `vw` and `vh`,
        are not accepted.

        Zero may be written without a unit. Negative values are accepted only
        where a property's entry says so.

    otherUnits:
        A few values are not sizes, and have units of their own:

        - deg
            Angles, as in `rotate: 45deg`.
        - fr
            A share of the free space in a grid track, as in
            `gridTemplateColumns: 1fr 2fr`.
        - numbers
            Plain numbers with no unit, for properties such as `opacity`,
            `flexGrow`, `lineHeight` and `scale`.
        - integers
            Whole numbers, for properties such as `zIndex` and `lineClamp`.

    colours:
        Wherever a property's entry lists `<color>`, the value is written in
        one of these four formats. Each is written exactly as CSS allows,
        including both the comma-separated and the space-separated forms of
        the functions, and out-of-range values are clamped as CSS clamps
        them.

        - hex
            A `#` followed by three, four, six or eight hexadecimal digits,
            such as `#fff`, `#ffff`, `#ffffff` or `#ffffff80`. The fourth or
            last pair of digits is the opacity.
        - rgba
            `rgba(red, green, blue, alpha)`, with each colour channel from 0
            to 255 or as a percentage, and alpha from 0 to 1 or as a
            percentage. `rgb()` is accepted as the same function, as in
            CSS.
        - hsl
            `hsl(hue, saturation, lightness)`, with hue in degrees and the
            other two as percentages, and an optional alpha. `hsla()` is
            accepted as the same function, as in CSS.
        - oklch
            `oklch(lightness chroma hue)`, written as in CSS, with an
            optional alpha after a slash.

        Named colours, such as `red`, and keywords such as `transparent` and
        `currentColor` are not accepted. A fully transparent colour is written
        as `#0000`.

    images:
        Wherever a property's entry lists an image, the value is
        `url(<path>)`, or a `linear-gradient()` or `radial-gradient()` written
        as in CSS. How gradients blend is set out in @{Layout}.

        A path is resolved the same way as an import path. It is relative to
        the file it is written in, or measured from the project's source root
        when it starts with `/`. It must point at a local file; remote URLs
        are not accepted, because the SVG and PNG renderers can't fetch them.
        The same rules apply to the `src` attribute of an image instance.

        Image files may be PNG, JPEG, WebP or SVG.

    keywords:
        Keywords, such as `flex`, `center` or `nowrap`, are written exactly as
        listed in each property's entry. They are case-sensitive.
